<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The sandbox is off, deliberately.

  Two things this app promises are incompatible with it:

  1. **The notes are ordinary files.** Under the sandbox, `~/Documents` is
     redirected into the app's container
     (`~/Library/Containers/com.jotit.jotit/Data/Documents`). A user who opens
     their vault folder in Finder sees nothing, and "here, edit your Markdown
     in any editor" stops being true. `path_provider` returns the container
     path either way, so the code cannot tell the difference — only the
     entitlement can.

  2. **The server is wherever the user put it.** A self-hosted WebDAV box on a
     LAN address is the normal case, and the sandbox would need
     `com.apple.security.network.client` granted by hand and a user consent
     flow for files.

  JotIt is distributed outside the Mac App Store, to people who run their own
  server; there is no untrusted-install threat the sandbox is protecting
  against that a signed, direct-download app does not already answer.

  What this costs, stated plainly: the app has the same file and network
  permissions as the user's own shell. It also means **no Hardened Runtime
  notarisation-friendly distribution** for the App Store — by design.

  If a store-ready build is ever wanted, the work is not flipping this back:
  it is `com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write` plus a folder
  picker, which is a different file API, not a different entitlement.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
	  The microphone, for recording a voice memo into a note.

	  The app is not sandboxed and the hardened runtime is not enabled, so
	  this entitlement is not what opens the microphone today — the usage
	  description in Info.plist is. It is here anyway, because the moment the
	  hardened runtime is turned on (which is what a notarised, distributable
	  build needs) a missing `device.audio-input` entitlement makes the
	  recording fail with no message at all. A build that cannot record is
	  not a build anybody notices until a user tries.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  The camera, for taking a picture into a note.

	  Same reasoning as the microphone above: it is not what opens the camera
	  today, but a missing `device.camera` entitlement under the hardened
	  runtime makes the capture fail silently, and a build that cannot take
	  a picture is not a build anybody notices until a user tries.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Reading a file the user picked in the open panel.

	  The app is not sandboxed, so this is not what lets the album picker
	  work today. It is here for the same reason as the camera: the day the
	  sandbox is turned on, a picker that cannot read what the user chose is
	  a picker that does nothing, and the failure is indistinguishable from
	  a broken app.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
